        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-5ec5a5c11bc4771e\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Rafa’s repeated “ti amo,” specifically “che ti amo da lunghi mesi, ogni giorno più forte,” is principally an avowal that his love is an enduring and intensifying emotional state. In answering Loretta’s fear that she will be replaced, it weakly implies continuing or exclusive attachment, but it does not clearly formulate a commitment; the surrounding courtship proposal and pleas do not by themselves establish strong performative force. Despite passionate repetition and urgency, the declaration forms part of an extended argument intended to persuade her, with no positive evidence that the words emerge as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is limited P pressure because the avowal reassures Loretta against replacement and supports a proposed continuing relationship, but its explicit content remains predominantly a report of Rafa’s feelings rather than a promise.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rafa directly addresses Loretta; the highlighted declaration is grammatically embedded in his rhetorical question (“Have you still not understood that I love you?”) and is immediately repeated and temporally elaborated.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rafa uses the avowal to rebut Loretta’s suspicion that his proposed arrangement is temporary and that another woman will replace her. The declaration therefore operates as persuasive reassurance within an ardent, self-interested courtship attempt. His claims that he can no longer reflect and fears no danger convey intensity, while his detailed reasoning, bargaining, and repeated request that she say yes make the exchange deliberative rather than reflexive. His subsequent physical pressure further complicates the scene ethically but does not alter the core utterance’s dominant avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Non hai compreso ancora che ti amo, che ti amo da lunghi mesi, ogni giorno più forte?”\",\"supports\":\"The temporal description of loving her for many months, increasingly strongly, explicitly presents an enduring emotional state as true and strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Loretta predicts that in spring “al posto di Lora se ne mette un’altra”; Rafa immediately replies, “Un’altra? Ma cosa dici?” and gives the repeated love declaration.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal is persuasive reassurance against anticipated replacement, supplying slight commitment-related pressure but not an explicit undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rafa continues: “Ma, ragiona un momento,” discusses the risks of his proposal, offers her what she wants, and repeatedly asks, “dimmi di sì!”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration belongs to a sustained argumentative and courtship appeal rather than being positively marked as a reflex-like verbal discharge; this supports E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration later describes Rafa’s “occhi appassionati” and “voce supplichevole” as he kneels and begs Loretta to love him a little.\",\"supports\":\"The scene establishes passionate, supplicatory delivery, but these contextual intensity cues do not independently establish expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression in Guido da Verona’s “Colei che non si deve amare.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted form is a present affirmative first-person love declaration rather than a negated or merely hypothetical expression.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the declaration’s dominant avowal of an enduring emotional state, while a low P score records its limited reassurance or relational implication. Passion, repetition, persuasion, and coercive context can be described contextually without requiring E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied analytical translation was used only as an aid; the evidential quotations are from the Italian source text.\"}"
        }
